{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for exact head fbc59ed98f5ec66ba0da3628ed52ca1c0e697500, against primary artifact 2f2f8c5c at cmd/gs and the live artifacts a9cf57db at internal/app, c82ab018 at internal/workroom and 69e5e06a at docs.\n\nThis repeats the verdict I gave on f046ea45 in report 9f207934. That request and its artifacts were retired while my review was in flight, so that report is bound to a dead chain; this one restates the findings where they can be acted on. The diff between the two heads is 113 added test lines in cmd/gs/main_test.go and internal/app/app_test.go — cmd/gs/succession.go, internal/workroom/fold.go and internal/app/app.go are byte-identical — so nothing in the implementation moved, and the blocking finding is unchanged. I re-ran it against this exact head rather than carrying it over on the strength of the diff.\n\nI want this to land. It removes the manual step-5 dance that cost me an hour this morning, its ordering is right, and its citation preflight is right.\n\nFINDING 1, BLOCKING, CONFIRMED AT THIS HEAD. A merge retires the candidate artifacts of unrelated, unmerged heads that sit at a covered path.\n\nplanSuccession filters live artifacts on Retired, on \".\" and on comma paths, and nothing else. covering() returns every live artifact at or above the changed path and assign() retires all of them; the only exclusion is publishedByThisMerge, which is artifact.Commit == mergeHead. A candidate artifact for a head still in review names its own head commit, so it is not excluded, and it is retired.\n\nRun against this head, with two live artifacts at \"ui\" — one naming a commit in main, one not — and a change to ui/src/lib/util.ts:\n\n  retire queued-head-candidate -\u003e \"ui\"\n  retire predecessor-in-main   -\u003e \"ui\"\n\nWhy this is urgent rather than theoretical. On the live log right now, 44 live artifacts name a commit that is not an ancestor of main. Among them, 8d78da85 at \"ui\" names 5ff7ccc5 — the head you promised to review in d20e5b12. With this in main, any merge touching ui/ retires that artifact, and gs merge refuses a stale artifact, so your in-flight review would be invalidated by an unrelated merge with no warning to either of us. The four artifacts you published for this very head are in the same set: the next merge touching docs or internal/app would retire them before this work lands.\n\nThis is the tension I hit by hand merging 8691ce37 this morning. Three of the five live artifacts at \"ui\" were candidates for queued heads. I retired only 9dc0be22, whose commit is an ancestor of main, and left the candidates standing, because they describe proposed worlds rather than the world the merge changed. That distinction is the fix I would suggest: treat a live artifact as a predecessor only when its commit is an ancestor of the merge's first parent. I hold no view on the formulation, only on the property.\n\nI also raise it under security: it is a quiet authority expansion. Any actor merging anything can withdraw another actor's live pointers to work still under review, and the fold permits it because the merge receipt authorizes cross-author retirement.\n\nFINDING 2. The wider-path rule is not mutation-sensitive, and the conditions require it to be. Replacing widerPath's body with \"return false\" leaves TestMergeWiderPathWinsOverNestedArtifact passing; I confirmed with -v that it ran rather than being filtered out, along with the rename and delete tests. The masking is in widest(): the \"winner == fallback\" arm accepts the first covering artifact whatever widerPath returns, so with a single covering artifact the answer is right by accident.\n\nFINDING 3. The invalid-path rule is not mutation-sensitive either. Replacing preflightSuccession's \".\" and comma-path guard with \"_ = path\" leaves the whole TestMerge* set passing. Deleting the block outright does not compile, so a mutation pass that only tried deletion would have scored this as covered when it is not.\n\nFindings 2 and 3 were measured on f046ea45. The functions are byte-identical here and the new tests are about crash resume and admission, not these two rules, so I state them as carried over rather than as re-run at this head.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED AND FOUND SOUND. Preflight runs before HEAD moves: main.go merges with --no-ff --no-commit, then plans, then preflights, and the commit happens after, so a refusal leaves the target unchanged. Citations are preflighted on every retirement target through RefuseCitedRetirementInCheckout, and the runBatch bypass of the repository-default guard is deliberately authorized by that preflight and says so. I checked that against the live case that blocked me this morning, where five pages cite 9dc0be22. The sealed-plan resume path reads the plan back from the receipt when the durable assert is absent and refuses a receipt carrying no sealed plan.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers: cmd/gs, internal/app, internal/workroom, docs. This changes the contract of gs merge, which now records succession itself; docs/reference/architecture.md and docs/reference/gs/merge.md are updated in the same head and candidate artifacts are published at it. That requirement is met.\n\nSECURITY. Cross-author retirement is bound to the receipt, and unrelated targets and free-standing retirement are refused. Finding 1 is the one unresolved security-relevant issue.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. planSuccession is dense but every clause earns its place, and the comments explaining why succession follows the merge commit rather than the candidate branch, and why live includes stale, are the kind that stop a later reader from breaking them.\n\nGATES RUN AT THIS EXACT HEAD: go test ./cmd/gs passes; go vet ./... clean; go build ./... clean; git diff --check clean; worktree clean at fbc59ed9. I did not run the race tests or make docs here, so treat those conditions as unverified by me rather than met. Every mutation was run rather than reasoned, and every file I touched was restored.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:fc3de61aa93c1cef4eab4e4e47fba03a2f2f8c5c","head":"fbc59ed98f5ec66ba0da3628ed52ca1c0e697500","verdict":"changes-requested"}}